A||||4||Four-Banger||Out in Colorado||||5||
Z||000000||Four-Banger||05-01-2001||11:14 AM||||It's been mentioned that in the future me and my better half might move to Colorado, the only thing is...we're scared to drive our fieros in all the snow.  Neither of us wants to give up our car, but we would both have very different work schedules.  So between the two of us, there would be four cars--two fieros and two vehicles which we would drive in the snow.  It sounds a bit much to me for two people to have four cars.  How does everyone else in Colorado handle the weather with their fiero?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||05-01-2001||01:04 PM||||Fieros do very well in all but the most severe snowstorms.  A good set of snow tires does wonders.....studded snows are great.....for a Fiero.  You might want to invest in a cheap set of wheel to have them mounted on so all you would need to do is bolt them on.  Its typical of the weather here to be cold and snowy one day and 65* the next.  Keeping those studded snows on for a long period of time doesn't pay.<P>LMK if/when you move here.......what part of Colorado are you looking at? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||05-01-2001||01:20 PM||||MAN I love the snow in my Fiero! Like most cars you just need to have a good set of boots on it. My Fiero is an absoulute joy to drive in the snow I have had her burried up the the rocker panels in snow and not had a problem. I have driven around stuck 4WD trucks! <BR>I will admit that I took the car out the very first snow I drove a Fiero in and went to a large empty parking lot and practiced driving, braking, turning, braking turns, Power turns , and just generaly trieing to get it out of control and recovering. It's just fun to drive. By comparison I have an 87 Subaru GL 4WD (I did the same test) and the Subaru is extriemly stable to the point of feeling like there was no snow. So its just a matter of your comfort level. My Fiero holds no supprises for me in snowy weather, I know exactly what it will do and when it will do it under most conditions (there are those hidden variables) <P>BTW I will sometimes bring my Fieros tire pressure down to 28 PSI depending on the amount of snow.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Four-Banger||05-01-2001||06:25 PM||||Well, seeing as how I have six years before I finish school, at the soonest it would be in about six years.  I would probably settle somewhere near Denver.  I just wasn't sure how a fiero would handle snow storms and such.  I live in Indianapolis now, and they keep the roads pretty clear when it snows.  I was just curious on what to do when there's so much snow that it practically falls in the car when you open the door to get in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||05-01-2001||06:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Four-Banger:<BR><B>I was just curious on what to do when there's so much snow that it practically falls in the car when you open the door to get in.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is what I do<P>1. Fire it up<BR>2. Clean it off<BR>3. Drive away<BR>4. point and laugh a lot<BR>||||reg||6
